## Allocation standard

Every billable resource must resolve to an owner, application, environment, and
cost center. Shared platform costs are allocated using the configured
**{{FACTORY-RAW:finops.chargebackModel}}** model. Untagged or ambiguously tagged
spend is assigned to the platform exception queue until corrected.

## The two budget mechanisms, and which one covers what

This estate has two budget records and they do different things. Reading one as
the other is the mistake this section exists to prevent.

#{{IF computed.defaultBudgetEnabled}}
### 1. The default subscription budget — DEPLOYED

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| Assigned at | {{FACTORY-RAW:computed.defaultBudgetGroupName}} |
| Amount per subscription | {{FACTORY-RAW:computed.defaultBudgetAmount}} |
| Period | {{FACTORY-RAW:computed.defaultBudgetTimeGrain}} |
| Notifies at | {{FACTORY-RAW:computed.defaultBudgetWarning}}% and {{FACTORY-RAW:computed.defaultBudgetCap}}% |
| Email contacts | {{FACTORY-RAW:computed.defaultBudgetContacts}} |
| Role contacts | {{FACTORY-RAW:computed.defaultBudgetRoles}} |

This one is real. `terraform/live/global` assigns the ALZ library's
`Deploy-Budget` policy at the management group above, and the policy creates a
`Microsoft.Consumption` budget on **every subscription beneath it**.

Two properties are worth being explicit about:

- **It covers subscriptions that do not exist yet.** `Deploy-Budget` is
  DeployIfNotExists, so a subscription vended into this hierarchy next year gets
  the budget without anyone rendering anything. A budget written as a Terraform
  resource would cover the subscriptions named at render time and silently miss
  every later one. That is the whole reason for preferring the policy.
- **A budget notifies. It does not cap.** Nothing stops at
  {{FACTORY-RAW:computed.defaultBudgetCap}}%; the threshold sends an alert and
  spend continues. Anyone reading "cap" as "limit" will be wrong at the moment
  it matters.

To change the amount, re-run the factory. Editing the budget by hand in the
portal is reverted the next time the policy remediates.
#{{ELSE}}
### 1. The default subscription budget — NOT ENABLED

No `Deploy-Budget` assignment was requested, so no subscription in this estate
carries a policy-created budget. The pinned ALZ library ships the policy
definition regardless — it is created at the tenant root and assigned nowhere —
so enabling it later is a factory answer, not new policy authoring.
#{{ENDIF}}

### 2. Recorded budgets — NOT deployed

